    "body": "Only 8% of procurement leaders surveyed have a defined plan for using artificial intelligence , according to ProcureAbility’s new 2026 State of Procurement Report . Another 68% are testing AI in selected areas without a formal plan, while 24% are still exploring possible uses.\nWhat’s Related\nProcureAbility released the findings on Oct. 7. Created with ProcureCon, the report draws on responses from 100 senior procurement leaders across multiple industries.\nThe survey also found that many organizations stil l have unfinished supply chain resilience plans. Seventy percent described their approach as still developing, while only 8% reported a comprehensive plan that is regularly updated and tested.\nThose findings come as respondents say their companies expect procurement to contribute more than savings. Sixty-nine percent said their organizations want both lower costs and broader business value, while 26% said procurement is expected to help drive revenue and innovation .\nRELATED STORIES: Why Procurement Teams Need Rules Before Giving AI More Power | Agentic AI Is Changing Procurement. Governance Must Keep Up\nWhat AI is being used for\nContract review and risk identification were the leading AI uses, cited by 58% of respondents. Analyzing purchasing spending and identifying opportunities within buying categories followed at 55%.\nThe obstacles were nearly as common. Forty-eight percent identified poor data quality or insufficient data infrastructure as the biggest problem in getting measurable value from AI, while 47% cited difficulty separating useful tools from overhyped products.\nThe small share with a defined AI plan had clear milestones and was actively putting that plan into practice. ProcureAbility President Darshan Deshmukh said procurement leaders need to connect AI spending with business priorities and measurable results.\nProcurement entering purchasing decisions too late\nThe report also examined when procurement enters purchasing decisions. Forty-five percent said their teams get involved before supplier discussions begin, but only 11% participate while the business case and purchasing requirements are being developed.\nAnother 39% said procurement enters after suppliers have already been identified, while 5% said it gets involved primarily during contract negotiations.\nProcureAbility said joining late can limit a team’s ability to influence requirements, compare alternatives, assess risk, and encourage competition among suppliers. Nineteen percent reported weak negotiating leverage because they enter too late and have few suppliers to choose from.\nResilience plans still being developed\nFor supply chain resilience, the report distinguished between plans under development and an approach that is already being tested and updated. Most respondents remained in the developing group.\n“Building resilience starts with visibility ,” Deshmukh said. He urged procurement leaders to examine risks beyond their direct suppliers and prepare to respond before those problems interrupt operations.",
